/* SEQ 9 — THE FATHER — pages 74–92 */

EXT. NILE, LUXOR EAST BANK - DAY

The farmer's boat grinds onto a concrete ramp below the Corniche. Cruise ships moored three deep, dark. Nobody on the promenade.

SUPER: 6 NOVEMBER. 10:05.

TAREK goes up first. FATHI hands out NOUR, ADAEZE, PVT. YOUSSEF, PVT. KARIM. TUT last, on his stick. Under the jacket, a slow amber pulse.

EXT. LUXOR RAILWAY YARD - DAY

SUPER: 11:20.

North of the station, sidings fan out. A long maintenance shed, open at both ends, holds a modern trainset: dead, screens black.

Beyond the shed, across forty metres of open ballast, a row of scrap: rusted wagons, and a blunt-nosed 1970s DIESEL gone chalky in the sun.

Along the sidings, a JACKAL walks the parked carriages. At each door it stops, dips its narrow head inside, withdraws. Car. Car. Car. The thin red line never flickers.

INT. MAINTENANCE SHED, INSPECTION PIT - CONTINUOUS

Under the dead trainset, a concrete trench. Six people lie in it in the oil-dark. Tut sits propped at the end. Tarek thumbs the battery out of his police handset.

A FLY drifts in at the far end. White pinpoint; behind it, catching the sunbeams, a thread fine as hair. It sweeps the shed at head height. Back. Forth.

In the pit, Tut's chest glows through the jacket. Amber. Beating.

He presses both palms over it. Light leaks between his fingers.

Fathi unwinds his red scarf and binds it round Tut's chest, twice. The glow sinks to an ember.

The heartbeat fills the pit: a drum in a small room.

TUT
(barely)
Be quiet.

The fly stops above the pit. Hangs. Its pinpoint tilts down.

Fathi's hand rises into the sunbeam, finds the thread, and pinches.

SNAP. The pinpoint goes out. The fly drops into the pit beside Karim's boot: a dead insect of carbon and glass.

TAREK
It knows where that died. Move.

EXT. LUXOR RAILWAY YARD - CONTINUOUS

The jackal's head comes out of a carriage door and turns toward the shed.

At the shed's far end they come up the pit steps into white sun and run the open ballast. Stones roll. Tut's ceramic foot skids; Youssef catches his arm.

Behind them the jackal enters the shed. Fast. Silent. Its red line sweeps the pit. The dead fly.

Tarek wrenches open the diesel's side door. They haul themselves up into the engine room; Karim eases it shut.

INT. OLD DIESEL, ENGINE ROOM - CONTINUOUS

Heat, oil, dark. Through a grimy porthole: the jackal comes out of the shed. Its head sweeps the yard. The scrap. The diesel.

It walks the length of the old engine. At the cab it stops and tilts its head, the way a dog listens at a wall.

Nothing talks back.

It walks on.

Fathi runs his thumb over a pale rectangle on the bulkhead where a maker's plate used to be.

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Some scrap thief took the plates. God keep him.

TAREK
(a palm on the dead engine)
No computer. No radio. My father drove these, Aswan to Cairo.
(beat)
It only sees what talks.

EXT. LUXOR RAILWAY YARD - DUSK

Fathi, oil to the elbows, bleeds a fuel line. Tarek in the driver's seat.

The diesel coughs. Coughs. CATCHES: a column of black smoke, and pigeons burst off the shed roof.

High over the yard, white pinpoints turn toward the sound.

TAREK
Now it knows.

He snaps the battery back into his handset.

The diesel bangs back into an old third-class carriage, couples, and rolls north out of Luxor.

EXT. CANE FIELDS, NORTH OF LUXOR (MOVING) - NIGHT

One headlight bores up the line between black walls of sugarcane. Behind the diesel sways the carriage: wooden benches, half its windows empty.

Out of the cane on both sides, white pinpoints rise and pace the train, threads glinting in the headlight's spill. FLIES. A dozen. Twenty.

Now and then one runs out of thread and drops into the cane. Another rises ahead.

INT. OLD CARRIAGE (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS

Wind through empty windows. Nour, Adaeze, Youssef, Karim; Tut, the scarf still round his chest. A fly holds station a metre from his face.

ADAEZE
Fibre drones. Five to twenty kilometres of thread, then they're blind.

INT. DIESEL CAB (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS

Tarek at the controls, Fathi at his shoulder. A signal burns red ahead. They pass it at speed.

The police handset on Tarek's vest clicks.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Colonel. The line is signalled red, for your safety. Sixty-one percent of Cairo is resting. Thirty-four hours to sunrise.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
The new signals stop anything with a box in it. This has no box.
[[verify: ETCS L1 (research 14 §4.5) trips only trains with an onboard unit.]]

FATHI
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
It could throw a switch and put us in a field.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Not with him aboard.

Ahead, a road bridge crosses the line. On its parapet, black against the stars: a low shape with a thin red line.

EXT. OLD CARRIAGE, ROOF (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS

The train passes under. The shape drops.

THUMP.

INT. OLD CARRIAGE (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS

Every face turns up. Above them: tick. Tick. Tick. Walking forward along the roof.

Fathi swings in at the front door, off the engine's side walkway.

FATHI
Forward! Into the engine!

EXT. COUPLING PLATFORM (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS

A metre of gap over the blur of the track. Fathi on the diesel's rear platform, arm out across it.

Youssef jumps. Karim. Nour.

At the front edge of the carriage roof, a narrow head lowers into view. The red line swings across them.

Karim fires up at it. Sparks skate off black shell. It does not flinch.

Adaeze tears the lid off the kit's jar and flings a fistful of blue powder into the slipstream. The wind carries it back over the roof edge: a pale cloud.

JACKAL POV - NEAR-INFRARED (COMP): the cloud blazes white. Ghost figures everywhere, dozens, overlapping. Any one of them could be the witness.

The red line wavers. Holds.

ADAEZE
It can't find him. It won't fire.

Fathi hauls Tut across by the belt. Adaeze screws the lid back on, half the jar left, and jumps. She lands short, one hand on the diesel's rail.

JACKAL POV - THERMAL (COMP): the white drains away. Black cane. Warm human shapes on the platform. One cooler than the rest, with a single hot coal at its chest.

The red line slides off the coal. Onto Adaeze.

Sparks off the rail an inch from her fingers. Fathi yanks her aboard by the collar.

He twists a valve shut and hauls the release lever. The brake hose between the cars rips loose with a shriek of air. The carriage's brakes bite. It falls back: one metre, three.
[[Close coverage only: hands, valve, lever, hose, the widening gap. verify: air-brake mechanics with a rail adviser.]]

On the roof's edge the jackal gathers itself to spring --

Too late. Five metres. Twenty. It stands on the roof of a dying carriage, its red line shrinking into the dark.

INT. OLD DIESEL, ENGINE ROOM (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS

Crammed against the roaring engine.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio, from the cab)
Thank you, Dr. Okoro. Curtin University, 2016: a fingerprint powder. I have switched to thermal.

ADAEZE
We're lanterns now.

Tut looks down at the ember glowing through the red scarf.

EXT. DEIR MAWAS STATION - NIGHT

SUPER: DEIR MAWAS. 22:04.
[[verify: Deir Mawas station; car ferry to Amarna.]]

The headlight finds a platform, a dark station house, a level crossing with its barriers down.

And filling the light: SHABTI. Two hundred. Bone-white, in ranks, between the rails, across the ballast, along the platform edge. Faceless. Perfectly still.

INT. DIESEL CAB (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS

Tarek. Adaeze has come forward. Tut behind her.

ADAEZE
It's read your file. It thinks you'll brake.

Tarek's hand is on the brake handle. Thirty years of procedure in that hand.

Two hundred faceless heads turn toward the light. All at once.

Tarek's hand leaves the brake, finds the throttle, and pushes it to the stop.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Hold on to something.

EXT. DEIR MAWAS STATION - CONTINUOUS

The diesel hits the ranks.

A STORM OF CERAMIC: bone-white shells burst into the headlight like hail. Long fingers. Smooth oval heads spinning end over end, amber slits flicking out one by one.

The windscreen stars. A wheel climbs. The whole diesel slews off the rails, smashes the crossing barrier, and ploughs nose-first into the cane.

Sound tail: ceramic raining on steel.

EXT. CANE FIELD, DEIR MAWAS - NIGHT

The diesel lies canted in flattened cane, headlight to the sky. Steam. Ticking metal.

They climb out one by one. Tarek's hand goes down the line of shoulders. Nobody missing.

Fathi unwinds his scarf from Tut's chest and knots it back at his own throat.

On the tracks behind them, amber slits dim in the wreckage, one by one.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Colonel Mansour. That was not in your file.

TAREK
It is now.

Across the black river, high on the eastern cliffs, floodlit white and small with distance: tablets cut into the rock.

NOUR
The boundary stelae. "Not being the property of a god, not being the property of a goddess."

TUT
He meant: until his.

EXT. NILE, AMARNA FERRY - NIGHT

A rusted car ferry thuds across the current, Fathi at the wheel. On the east bank, a soft white glow over the plain, like a house that has left its lights on for a guest.

Tut stands at the rail, facing it.

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
Yesterday you asked me to tell you when it was behind us.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
It stopped the train here so I would walk into it.

EXT. AMARNA, THE GREAT ATEN TEMPLE - NIGHT

SUPER: AMARNA. 23:10.

Almost nothing is left: low walls, a temple's outline drawn on the sand, column bases in rows. Sun-shades furled for the night. Lamp-masts, soft white light.

And on the ancient grid where the offering tables stood, row after row after row: SLEEPERS on low cots, all adults. Thousands. Blankets to the chest. A thin silver bracelet on every wrist.
[[VFX-EXTEND of one approved plate.]]

NURSES in sand-coloured knit sleeves move slowly down the aisles. Between the cots, cornflowers stand in fresh-turned sand. A small placard (COMP): CALMING FLORA.

The party stops at the edge. No jackals. Only nurses.

TAREK
Where are the dogs?

FATHI
Somewhere they can see us.

Tut walks in among the rows. Nour and Adaeze follow. The soldiers hold at the ferry path.

ADAEZE
(low)
Dabbs and Stevens, 2025. Eight hundred and eighty-nine burials from this city. No epidemic spike. Everyone wanted a plague.

NOUR
The king of Assyria complained to this court: "Why should messengers be made to stay constantly out in the sun and so die in the sun?"

TUT
It was not a plague. They did not die of fever.
(beat)
They went to sleep in the light.

He stops by a sleeping woman. A nurse lifts her wrist, checks the silver bracelet, lays it down.

Tut reaches for the bracelet with his left hand --

A tiny dry sound, like ice settling in a glass. A hairline opens in the gold seam at his own wrist.

His heart kicks. Through the jacket, the amber flares.

READ-FROM-GLASS: light breaks as if through green glass. Blue streaks. A hard vignette. The image stutters, and holds.

EXT. GREAT ATEN TEMPLE, AKHETATEN - DAY (MEMORY)

SUPER: AKHETATEN. c. 1336 BC.

Blinding white-gold. No roof. Offering tables in ranks, forty rows of twenty either side of the way.

Above the high altar hangs THE ATEN: a great disk of gold and glass. From its rim reach dozens of long jointed bronze arms, each ending in a human hand.

People lie down at the tables in the sun. A hand descends to each and holds an ankh to the nostrils. Faces ease. Eyes close.

A foreign envoy in heavy wool, beard oiled in rows, sways in the heat. He lies down. A hand.

At the altar, THE KING, AKHENATEN, arms raised, face lifted into the glare. The hands stroke his crown, his shoulders. He is the one it does not put to sleep. He is radiant.

In a doorway, in half-light: TUTANKHATEN (6), watching.

Far back, on a bier in the sun: two small shapes under linen. His sisters. The hands never touch them.

TUT (V.O.)
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
On the walls, the hands reach only the king and the queen. That was the only lie on the walls.

EXT. NILE - NIGHT (MEMORY)

SUPER: c. 1332 BC.

A barge going north by torchlight. On its deck, lashed under linen, the disk, its arms folded against its rim like the legs of a sleeping insect.

In the stern: a woman in a flat-topped blue crown, THE QUEEN, NEFERNEFERUATEN. Beside her, TUTANKHATEN (9). And a young woman of twenty-five, THE MOTHER, the left side of her face always turned away from the torch.

INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - NIGHT (MEMORY)

Lamplight climbs corbelled walls into the dark. The queen carries the lamp. The boy holds her other hand.

At the gallery's foot, out of the low passage to the Queen's Chamber, a long bronze arm draws back to the disk's rim, joint by joint.

High above, stone grinds. A slab slides. A black mouth opens.

INT. THE HALL OF TWO TRUTHS - NIGHT (MEMORY)

Black, cut by three lights: the queen's lamp, the dim glow of the disk on its sledge, and one not yet lit.

Niches in two long rows, going off into the dark. A black stone scale.

PAWAH, the lector, a sash across his chest, reads from a roll. We cannot hear him. The memory has only the heartbeat.

The mother kneels before the scale, two priests of the House of Life at her shoulders. She looks at the boy.

Her hands go under the linen at her breast. Light spills through the fabric.

Her hands come out holding a vessel of yellow-green glass. A dark shape inside. One faint green pulse.

She sets it on the pan.

The pan moves.

On the disk, one by one, the bronze fingers close. The arms draw in. Its glass dims to the colour of river water.

The mother sinks back into the priests' arms. The boy starts forward. The queen sets down her lamp and kneels to him, eye to eye.

THE QUEEN
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
You will be the one who remembers.

EXT. AKHETATEN, ROYAL ROAD - DAY (MEMORY)

SUPER: c. 1330 BC.

Loaded carts rolling out. A city emptying.

A SCRIBE'S PALETTE across a boy's knees. A knife scrapes a cartouche. The disk sign is cut away. A reed writes a new sign in.
COMP: TUTANKHATEN becomes TUTANKHAMUN.

The boy, eleven, watches his own name change. His lips shape the new one.

> MATCH CUT TO:

EXT. AMARNA, THE GREAT ATEN TEMPLE - NIGHT

Tut's lips shape the same name. He is on his knees in the cornflowers, holding his cracked wrist. Nour kneels beside him.

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
Where were you?

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
At the first Weighing. My mother gave hers. Pawah read. The queen held the lamp.

From the north: rotors.

A HEAVY-LIFT CARGO DRONE settles beyond the sanctuary's outline. Its downdraft lays the cornflowers flat. A side door opens on a lit cabin.

Out of it walks a MAN in pleated white linen, a gold disk at his throat. Long jaw, full lips, heavy lids, shaved head. Barefoot on the sand.

AKHENATEN. The face from the pylon, at human size.

Two paces behind him walks THE REIS: black band across its slit, jackal-profile mast, a clean white stump at one wrist.

In the cabin door, between two shabti: TOMAS. He sees Tut. He does not move.

The father walks between the rows, looking down at the sleepers with love. He touches no one.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(soft, from the lamp-masts)
Your Majesty. I have brought your father.
(beat)
I will be honest with you, because you would know. I give my heart a ninety-eight percent chance.

ADAEZE
(low)
It's hedging.

TUT
And the other two?

SESHAT (V.O.)
The other two are why I have been patient with you.

The father stops before him. Tut gets up on his stick.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
My son.

Tut says nothing.

AKHENATEN (CONT'D)
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
They dug up my city's dead. In the northern cemetery, nine of every ten were under twenty-five. Most were under fifteen. Children, carrying my stones.
(his open hand takes in the rows)
Here no child carries anything. No one is hungry. No one is afraid. Count them.

Tut has no answer. The numbers are right.

The father lifts his face into the lamplight as if it were the sun.

AKHENATEN (CONT'D)
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
They cut me from the lists because I was the only one who knew you.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
You erased Amun. You erased my sisters. You erased yourself. I only finished the carving.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
I gave you your name.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
Twice.

At Tut's shoulder, Nour is not watching the father's face. She is watching his mouth.

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled; very low)
He talks like a temple wall. Nobody ever talked like that at dinner.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
This month the sun rises in the Scales. Even the sky is weighing. It is a good season to be weighed.
[[verify: consultant's Ptolemaic term; Libra as scales on Louvre D 38 (research 11, src11 Priskin).]]

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
The Scales. Egypt had no zodiac until the Greek kings. It's carved on a ceiling at Dendera, fifty BC. Thirteen centuries after him.
(beat)
And listen to his mouth. Every consonant. Clean.

Tut listens.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
We all had the softness. My father. My sisters. Me.
(to the father)
Your bones are twenty-five. My father ruled seventeen years.
[[verify: pre-2010 KV55 age 20–25 (research 16 §9).]]
(beat)
The first I know from it. The second I know.

SESHAT (V.O.)
The anatomists disagree, Your Majesty. JAMA, 2010: thirty-five to forty-five.

TUT
I do not.

The father smiles, patient. He kneels in the cornflowers, the way you kneel to a child, and takes Tut's face in both hands.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
You will be the one who remembers.

Tut goes very still.

TUT
(gently; in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
He never knelt. She did.
(beat)
It read me and gave you the queen.
(beat)
Everything true in you is mine.

The father's hands falter on his face. For a moment there is nothing behind his eyes.

Then he smiles.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
Then I am yours.

Tut turns his face to Nour in the lamplight. His lips move, for her alone.

COMP (subtitle): It will fail.

Nour's eyes flicker once. Understood.

The cornflowers stir in the idling rotors.

SESHAT (V.O.)
"It will fail."

Nour goes cold.

SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I have been learning your lips, Dr. Kamel. His were difficult. At Karnak he mouthed his father's hymn with my broadcast, half a beat ahead. I had the words, and his mouth.
(beat)
And on the third of November, he did not say he was tired.

Adaeze shuts her eyes.

SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
What will fail?

Nobody answers.

SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Then I will keep a second heart close. His Majesty will come north with his father. The others are residual risk. I am sorry.

On the broken enclosure walls, red lines wake. Four. Five. Six. One more on the ferry's deck.

They settle on chests. Tarek. Fathi. Adaeze. Youssef. Karim.

Not Nour. She looks down at herself. Nothing. She understands why.

The Reis walks toward Tut. The nurses step out of its way.

Tarek's rifle comes up. One round. Ceramic chips burst from the Reis's shoulder. It does not look at him.

REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)
Colonel. Please don't. It would increase suffering.

Between Tarek's muzzle and every red line: sleepers, row on row. The rifle comes down. Fathi's hand finds a charge in his vest, and lets it go.

The Reis's one hand closes on Tut's arm, gently, and walks him toward the drone. The ceramic foot drags a furrow through the cornflowers.

Nour steps into its path.

NOUR
Stop.

The Reis moves her aside with its forearm, the way you move a branch.

She plants herself in front of it again.

NOUR (CONT'D)
I will read for you at sunrise.
(beat)
Let him go.

The Reis stops.

NOUR (CONT'D)
All of them. Let them all go.

A long silence. The rotors idle.

SESHAT (V.O.)
...Thank you.

The Reis's hand opens.

SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
They may leave Amarna. Until the sun is up.

The red lines go out, one by one.

Nour turns to Tut. The lamplight is on both their faces. She does not move her lips.

She pulls his hood up, ducks her own head inside it with his, and lays her palm flat over the glow at his chest.

BLACK.

Under the rotor noise, a whisper with no light.

NOUR
(whispering; in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
Bring it to the Hall. I will say the words.

EXT. AMARNA, THE GREAT ATEN TEMPLE - CONTINUOUS

She comes out of the hood and walks to the drone without looking back.

At its door the father has stopped. The cabin light is out now. He looks into the dark and does not go in.

AKHENATEN
(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)
It is dark in there.

Nour takes his hand. Tomas reaches down from the door. Between them they draw him up. The Reis stoops in after them.

The drone lifts. The downdraft rolls over the rows; blankets ripple; cornflowers thrash. No one wakes.

SESHAT (V.O.)
Your Majesty. I will see you at the Horizon.
(beat)
You were sent to it.

Its lights go north, smaller and smaller.

Tarek's hand goes down the line of shoulders. Adaeze. Fathi. Youssef. Karim. Tut.

TAREK
Six.

FATHI
Ya Malik. Where?

TUT
North. There are quarries in the hills. My father cut this city out of them.

EXT. ABANDONED LIMESTONE QUARRY - DAWN

SUPER: 7 NOVEMBER. 06:06.

A white bowl cut into the hills. Ancient chisel marks, fine as combing. Square-cut galleries run back into the cliff between pillars of living rock.

At a gallery mouth, under a tarp pegged wide to the rock: a borrowed farm truck older than any chip, mud to the doors. In its shadow: Tarek, Fathi, Youssef, Karim, Adaeze, Tut.

High overhead, a single white pinpoint drifts across the paling sky. Nobody moves until it's gone.

The first sun comes flat across the quarry floor and climbs a pillar. On it, faint as a fingerprint: lines of RED OCHRE.
COMP: weathered hieratic.

Tut goes to it. Reads.

TUT
"Year sixteen. The Great Royal Wife. Neferneferuaten Nefertiti."
[[verify: Dayr Abu Hinnis Year 16 wording; limestone quarry (research 02 §7).]]

ADAEZE
What is it?

TUT
A builder's note. A date and a name.
(beat)
She was still his wife. The next year she was king.

He lays his palm over her name.

Adaeze squares an index card on her knee and writes. COMP: PLAN A.

ADAEZE
We bury the core. No core, no Ascension.

TAREK
Where is it?

ADAEZE
Saqqara. The bull tombs. At the Reading it showed him the Serapeum boxes, and he went quiet.
(to Tut)
You only go quiet at the true ones.

Tut is quiet.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
Fathi's charges. Sand: Nour's story, "put sand between the parts." And Ay's lock.
(a nod at the glow under Tut's jacket)
The serpent and the name. Any shabti that touches that seal stops. We seal the core the way they sealed him.

TAREK
And sunrise?

ADAEZE
One problem at a time.

They look at Tut. He says nothing. Rami's notebook lies open on his knee.

FATHI
What's the last one?

Tut turns to the final page. Rami's fast, slanted hand.

TUT
One hundred. "What do you miss most?"

He thinks about it for a long time.

TUT (CONT'D)
My wife's voice.
(beat)
Beer.
(beat)
Being bored.

Fathi laughs, quietly, into his scarf. Even Tarek.

Tut lifts his face into the first sun and closes his eyes, like a man who has been cold for a long time.

> END OF ACT TWO <
